# Airport Emergency Management Director — Stage 1 Brief

**Run:** quiet-by-design-standards-for-mwaa-terminals-2
**Chair:** Director of Emergency Management, top-tier US hub (25 years in seat; prior large-hub tenure)
**Written:** 2026-08-07
**Assignment lens:** Does a MWAA quiet-by-design terminal standard strengthen or erode operational emergency-management capability at IAD and DCA?

---

## Frame before findings

Emergency management at a large US hub is not the binder on the shelf. It is the person who takes the 2:47 AM call, decides in the first four minutes whether to activate, and knows without reading a phone list which airline duty manager, TSA FSD deputy, MWAA Airports Authority Police watch commander, county EM duty officer, and FBI JOC line to raise. Quiet-by-design is, on its face, an architectural and acoustics question. It is not read that way from the EOC chair. From this chair it is a question about **whether the terminal, at the moment of a no-notice event, can still push the right words to the right people in the right zones with the right redundancy** — and whether the governance that gets us there preserves or fragments the operational authority to do it.

The short answer: **the acoustics half of the thesis strengthens EM capability. The governance half is where it can quietly erode it.** A quiet terminal, correctly commissioned, is a more intelligible terminal — signal-to-noise is the master variable in emergency voice communication. But quiet-by-design programs at other hubs have tended to migrate PA-push authority away from the airport operations duty officer and into vendor-managed content platforms, and that is exactly the wrong tradeoff for the first 30 minutes of an activation. The standard has to be written by someone who has run an EOC, not just someone who has commissioned a sound system.

---

## 1. Key findings — a peer EM director would recognize these as true

1. **Ambient reduction is an EM win, not an EM cost.** NFPA 72 sets emergency voice intelligibility at an STI floor of 0.45 at 90% of measurement points with a 0.50 average per acoustically distinguishable space (ADS). Every hub concourse I have measured misses that threshold not because the loudspeakers are bad but because the ambient noise is too high. A quiet-by-design specification that pushes background NC/RC down and reverberation time into a controlled band directly raises STI. That is a hard operational win for evacuation, shelter-in-place, and active-assailant messaging.

2. **PA zoning is table-stakes for granular mass notification — and a live governance risk.** Zoned voice systems let an EOC evacuate one concourse, shelter-in-place another, and leave a CBP FIS undisturbed. That is exactly the kind of granularity the FLL 2017 response wanted and did not have. But zoning multiplies the number of humans and systems that can push audio to a zone. If the standard does not name a single position — Airport Duty Manager or EOC watch officer — with pre-authorized override, the zoning benefit is neutralized the first time two people try to talk at once.

3. **The "quiet" script is where most programs quietly downgrade EM capability.** Schiphol, Helsinki, and London City reduced routine PA and shifted to visual-first information. In every case I have seen replicated in the US, the reduction touched routine announcements first and then, without an explicit carve-out, drifted into curtailing "airport information" announcements that the operations duty officer actually uses during IROPS, gate-hold changes, and slow-boil incidents. The standard needs a hard rule: emergency, security, and operationally directed announcements are not part of the volume budget.

4. **Redundant channels are not optional; they are what let you activate with confidence.** ADA, Section 504, and the ACAA require accessible information — but the EM case for redundancy is stronger than the compliance case. Every real activation I have run pushed the same message across at least three channels (PA, FIDS override, airline app push, staffed floor-walkers, and where present digital signage and social). Take any one of those away and the first 30 minutes get slower. The MWAA standard must specify all of them as first-class, not audio-first with digital backup.

5. **The activation matrix and the actual call tree diverge, and the standard has to design for the call tree.** The matrix says the EOC activates when X + Y + Z. In practice the duty officer activates when their gut says the event is not stabilizing, and then they call people by name, not position. That is why 25-year tenures matter. A quiet-by-design standard that assumes decisions flow through position-holders reading scripts will not survive first contact. The system spec must let a named human, on the phone from a car, push the right zone the right message in under 90 seconds without a service ticket.

6. **DCA's threat and consequence profile is different enough from IAD's to require an explicit two-airport variance clause.** DCA is inside the National Capital Region, adjacent to the Pentagon, in the SFRA, with a single main runway, slot-driven schedule, and — as of January 29, 2025 — a live open NTSB investigation into a Class A midair with 67 fatalities. IAD is a Cat X international hub with CBP FIS, mobile lounge legacy, and a decade-long transformation ahead. A single-standard, single-implementation approach is architecturally clean and operationally wrong. The tiered standard the prompt asks for has to hold at MWAA-common, and vary hard at terminal-level.

7. **The trend line is toward EM as a director-level function, not a coordinator role tucked under operations.** Post-9/11 the function got professionalized; post-Sandy and Hurricane Ian, continuity of operations became a board-visible line item; COVID and the July 19, 2024 CrowdStrike outage turned "cyber and IT continuity" into peer disciplines to weather. Fifteen years ago, EM at a hub was often a captain-rank position reporting three levels down. Today at peer hubs it reports to a Chief Operating Officer or, at the strongest programs, has direct escalation to the CEO during activations. Any standard that MWAA writes will be executed by whoever holds that chair five years from now. Design it for the seat, not the incumbent.

8. **Grant-funded capability is real but fragile.** UASI (National Capital Region UASI is one of the wealthier urban area allocations in the country and MWAA is inside the recipient geography), FEMA EMPG, and BIL/IIJA hazard mitigation funds have quietly built EM capability at hubs over the last decade. But those are the first line items cut when the local match tightens. A quiet-by-design capital line item that folds EM notification hardware into the base building spec — not into an annual grant chase — is one of the most durable things this thesis could actually deliver.

