## 2. Evidence

**Statutory and doctrinal baseline.** 14 CFR § 139.325 requires every Part 139 certificate holder to develop and maintain an Airport Emergency Plan that includes "procedures for prompt response to all emergencies … including a communications network" and to conduct a full-scale exercise at least every 36 months [Source: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-I/subchapter-G/part-139/subpart-D/section-139.325]. The rule is explicit on communications capability, not just on procedural coverage. FAA Advisory Circular 150/5200-31C (consolidated with Change 2) is the authoritative guidance for building that plan and is the document the FAA Part 139 inspector will read against MWAA's AEP [Source: https://www.faa.gov/documentlibrary/media/advisory_circular/150_5200_31c_consolidated.pdf]. Neither the CFR nor the AC specifies acoustic performance for the notification system — that is deferred to NFPA 72 and to the airport's own design standards. That deferral is exactly the gap a quiet-by-design standard can close.

**Emergency voice intelligibility standard.** NFPA 72 (National Fire Alarm and Signaling Code) requires that emergency communication systems achieve an STI (Speech Transmission Index) of not less than 0.45 at 90% of measurement locations in each acoustically distinguishable space, with an average STI of not less than 0.50 [Source: https://sdifire.com/ensuring-clear-communication/]. STI is dominated by two variables the architect controls: reverberation time and background noise. Every quiet-by-design specification that pulls ambient down and RT60 into the target band pulls STI up, and every one that ignores background noise and specifies only more speakers wastes money and degrades intelligibility. This is the technical bridge from a "quiet" thesis to an EM capability gain.

**Fort Lauderdale-Hollywood, January 6, 2017.** Five killed, six wounded in a shooting at baggage claim in Terminal 2, followed by a mass self-evacuation onto the airfield and hours of communications and command failure. The Police Executive Research Forum after-action report, commissioned by Broward County Aviation Department, describes radio interoperability breakdowns, incompatible communications between BSO and airport operations, and a mass-notification gap that let unverified reports of a second shooter drive tarmac evacuations across the airfield [Source: https://www.policinginstitute.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/Ft.-Lauderdale-Hollywood-International-Airport-AAR.pdf]. The corrective action items that mattered most were about who could push what message to which zone, in what sequence, on what network — not about signage or acoustics. FLL is the reference activation for why PA governance is the load-bearing element of any quiet standard.

**Hartsfield-Jackson Atlanta, December 17, 2017.** A Georgia Power switchgear fire in an underground tunnel took out both feeds to the airport, including the redundant substation, and grounded the world's busiest airport for roughly 11 hours; more than 1,000 flights were cancelled and ~30,000 passengers were affected [Source: https://www.cnn.com/2017/12/17/us/atlanta-airport-power-outage/index.html]. The operational EM lesson was not the fire — it was that the PA and FIDS systems went with the power, and airport staff had to walk terminals with megaphones and paper signs. Any quiet-by-design standard that does not spec emergency notification on generator-backed UPS with independent battery for the head-end and speakers has learned the wrong lesson from ATL 2017.

**DCA January 29, 2025 midair.** A PSA Airlines Bombardier CRJ700 operating as PSA 5342 collided with a US Army Sikorsky UH-60 on approach to DCA, killing all 67 aboard both aircraft. The NTSB investigation, concluded January 27, 2026, cited multiple systemic failures including FAA's placement and non-review of the helicopter route and the fact that a tower call describing the CRJ as "circling to runway 33" was likely stepped on and inaudible to the Black Hawk crew [Source: https://www.npr.org/2026/01/27/nx-s1-5689091/ntsb-dca-midair-collision-black-hawk-helicopter]. The incident is doubly relevant here: it is the reason DCA's 2025 traffic dropped 5.3% to 24.9M and it is a live reminder that DCA sits in the highest-consequence operational environment in the US airport system. The quiet-by-design standard is not being written into a benign environment at DCA.

**Guidance on airport emergency notification and terminal incident response.** ACRP Report 88, *Guidebook on Integrating GIS in Emergency Management at Airports*, and ACRP Report 112, *Airport Terminal Incident Response Planning* (with its Terminal Incident Response Plan tool), are the two most operationally useful ACRP products for the notification and terminal-response side of this thesis [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/catalog/22333/airport-terminal-incident-response-planning]. Both predate the current PA-zoning and mass-notification technology curve but their governance frameworks — who owns the plan, who commissions the system, who exercises it — remain the standard.

**MWAA context that matters.** MWAA governance flows through a 17-member board with a president and CEO and separate airport managers at IAD and DCA; the Airports Authority Design Manual is the natural home for a codified quiet-by-design and emergency notification standard, and the public record does not currently disclose explicit acoustic or STI performance requirements inside those volumes [Source: outputs/context/airport-context.md, §2 and §8]. The 2025 15-year Use & Lease and its $9.0B CCP, plus the July 2026 announcement of a $20–22.5B Dulles transformation, put MWAA into the widest terminal-design procurement window it will have this generation [Source: outputs/context/airport-context.md, §3 and §5]. Standards written now enter design manuals before consultant scopes and RFPs harden, which is the only leverage point that actually shapes what gets built.

**Evidence gap flagged, not filled.** I did not identify a public MWAA after-action report, exercise report, or PA governance document that would let me score the current state of MWAA emergency communications capability. That is not proof of a weakness — mature EM programs often keep AARs internal — but it is a fact the standard-writing process will have to reckon with by pulling the internal record. Recommend the strategist name this as an open item, not paper over it.

