## 3. The preparedness case for and against the thesis

**For (strengthens EM capability).** A quiet-by-design terminal is a more intelligible terminal. STI rises when ambient drops. A zoned voice system with pre-authorized single-position override lets an EOC contain a hold-room incident to that hold-room without dumping the whole concourse onto the ramp. A redundant, multi-channel information architecture (PA + FIDS + app + signage + staff) is exactly what the FLL 2017 AAR wished the response had. A capital-funded emergency notification specification, built into the base building through the Design Manual, decouples EM notification capability from the annual grant cycle. The activation that most directly supports this case is FLL 2017: every corrective action of consequence was about the ability to push differentiated messages to differentiated zones on differentiated networks. Quiet-by-design, done correctly, forces that engineering into the base design instead of bolting it on later.

**Against (erodes EM capability).** Quiet-terminal programs at peer hubs — the ones actually observed rather than the ones in the marketing decks — have twice migrated PA authority from the operations duty officer to a vendor-managed content platform, on the argument that content moderation was needed to keep the terminal quiet. In each case the operational effect was a slower first push during activation, because the person authorized to write and release the message was not the person answering the 2:47 AM call. A second failure mode: PA volume budgets that get enforced across all announcement categories rather than carving out emergency, security, and operationally directed messages. Both are governance failures, not acoustics failures. The activations that make this case: ATL 2017, where the PA went dark with the substation and the standard had never contemplated head-end battery; and CrowdStrike Day (July 19, 2024), where hubs discovered that IT-managed notification stacks failed simultaneously with the airline check-in systems and airport operations centers had to fall back to voice, radio, and floor-walkers. If the MWAA quiet standard puts more of the notification stack under managed IT and none of it under a duty-officer analog fallback, it is worse than the current state.

**Net.** The thesis strengthens capability if and only if (a) emergency, security, and operationally directed communications are carved out of the volume budget in writing; (b) PA-push authority is anchored to a named airport position with pre-authorized override and no service-ticket gating; (c) the notification head-end, IDF/MDFs feeding the audio bus, and the speakers themselves are specified on generator + UPS + local battery, with a red-line loss-of-comms drill that the design must survive; and (d) the standard is co-written by MWAA's EM lead, not delivered as a completed acoustics deliverable for review.

