## 4. Anchor points a strategist can use

1. **FLL 2017 active-shooter response, PERF/BCAD after-action report.** The reference activation for PA governance, radio interoperability, and mass-notification zoning. Use as the load-bearing case for why zoning without governance is worse than no zoning. [Source: https://www.policinginstitute.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/Ft.-Lauderdale-Hollywood-International-Airport-AAR.pdf]

2. **ATL December 17, 2017 power outage.** The reference activation for why emergency notification cannot share fate with commercial power or with the primary FIDS network. Anchor the "generator + UPS + local battery" requirement here. [Source: https://www.cnn.com/2017/12/17/us/atlanta-airport-power-outage/index.html]

3. **DCA January 29, 2025 midair and NTSB final, January 27, 2026.** The reference activation for the current DCA operating environment and the reason any two-airport standard has to hold a real variance for DCA. Also the strongest local argument for why MWAA's EM function is under-resourced relative to its consequence profile. [Source: https://www.npr.org/2026/01/27/nx-s1-5689091/ntsb-dca-midair-collision-black-hawk-helicopter]

4. **NFPA 72 STI 0.45/0.50 threshold and FAA AC 150/5200-31C (Change 2).** The two authoritative documents the MWAA quiet standard has to be legible against. Use to shift the argument from "how quiet" to "how intelligible under load," which is the language a Part 139 inspector, a fire marshal, and an accessibility auditor all recognize. [Source: https://sdifire.com/ensuring-clear-communication/] [Source: https://www.faa.gov/documentlibrary/media/advisory_circular/150_5200_31c_consolidated.pdf]

5. **CrowdStrike Day, July 19, 2024, at major hubs.** The reference activation for why the emergency notification stack cannot ride entirely on managed IT. Anchor the "duty-officer analog fallback" requirement — the person on the phone in the car has to be able to push the message with the systems still standing, whichever ones those are on the day.

---

## Judgment, marked as judgment

The thesis, as written, is the most useful piece of terminal design work MWAA could do for EM in this generation — but only if the EM director sits at the table when the standard is drafted, not when it is presented for signoff. The single failure mode I would bet on if that does not happen is PA-push authority migrating quietly from the operations duty officer to a vendor content-management platform. That is not an acoustics decision; it is a governance decision made inside an acoustics document, and it will not be caught by anyone reviewing the deliverable as an architecture product.

The other bet I would make: the standard will be adopted at MWAA-common, will hold at IAD through the Dulles transformation, and will fracture at DCA the first time an incident-driven flight restriction requires a terminal-wide operational message that the "quiet" volume budget doesn't accommodate. Build the DCA variance now, in writing, or watch it get built by exception later.

Twenty-five years in this seat has taught me that the standards that actually shape real activations are the ones written before anyone can imagine the activation that will test them. The strategist should push MWAA to write this one now, and to write it with the EM chair holding the pen on the notification, zoning, and authority provisions.
